{#
  Started as a fully standalone design experiment with no links in — now
  also the destination of "Continue editing" on
  /v5/manage-guidance/document-overview.html, at
  /v5/editor-experiment?id=<a guidance-documents.js id>. documentTitle/
  editorSections (app/routes.js) replace the page heading and every
  section here with that document's own title/content when a real id
  matches; a direct visit with neither set (no ?id=, or one matching
  nothing) still renders the exact same fixed "SFI 23 Guidance document"
  sample this page always has — see the {% if editorSections %} branch
  further down. Comments/Checks in the Changes panel stay hardcoded sample
  data regardless — not part of this change, per the task it was built
  from — so this route does not touch session data either way, only
  guidance-documents.js/generic-guidance-content.js when editorSections is
  actually being built.

  Layout: Content sidebar (left) and a single bordered box (right) split
  into an editor pane and a Changes pane, divided by one vertical border
  rather than two separate boxes — this was already true before the
  "merge Comments/Checks into one container" update (.app-experiment-box
  has always been the one shared border; .app-experiment-changes'
  border-left is the only internal divider), so that update changed
  nothing about the box/border CSS itself, only how the Comments tab's own
  content is laid out inside it — see the floating-comments comment below.
  Comments and Checks are two tabs inside the Changes pane — hand-rolled
  rather than govukTabs(), since that macro pipes each panel through
  Nunjucks' indent() filter (a known issue with markdown/code-like content
  elsewhere in this prototype) and these tabs also need a different look
  (underline, not boxed) to the ones used elsewhere, so reusing that
  markup pattern would not fit anyway.

  Clicking a comment's header still highlights the *section* it's linked
  to (data-target-section) and scrolls the editor pane there — unchanged.
  Every comment now always renders fully expanded (thread and reply box
  both permanently visible, no collapse/reveal step) — see the Comments
  panel comment further down for what that replaced.

  The sidebar's active item (Overview) is a fixed example of what that
  state looks like, not something the page keeps in sync with real scroll
  position — nothing here reads scroll position for the *nav* at all
  (only the Comments panel's floating positions do, see below).

  Full width: govuk-width-container (from the kit's own template.njk, not a
  file this page can touch) caps and centres everything passed to the
  content block, so .app-experiment-page uses the standard "break out via
  100vw" trick to escape it regardless of that container's own max-width,
  rather than trying to override govuk-width-container itself.

  No fixed height or internal scrolling on .app-experiment-box or either
  pane inside it: the editor content and the Changes panel both grow with
  their own content and scroll together as part of the ordinary page
  scroll — an earlier version gave the box a viewport-relative height with
  each pane scrolling independently inside it, which cut comments off and
  broke the floating-comment tracking below, since a card's position was
  being calculated against a scroll container that wasn't the one
  actually moving. See .app-experiment-box in
  _app-editor-experiment.scss and computeFloatingPositions() further down
  for how position tracking works now that both columns share one scroll.

  Editable content: [data-editor-content] is contenteditable and the
  Tiptap-styled toolbar above it drives it with document.execCommand (see
  wireToolbar). The section divs and ids
  inside the content are unchanged, so the sidebar's plain #section-x
  anchor links and the comment highlight-and-scroll logic both still work
  exactly as before; contenteditable only affects typing and selection
  inside the text, not clicks on the sidebar or the comments list. Each
  section is
  preceded by a app-experiment-boundary marker — dragging (or arrow-keying)
  one now really does move paragraphs between the two sections it sits
  between, see wireBoundaryHandle further down; "Add new content" appends
  a brand new, empty one at the very end, after everything else here.
#}
{% extends "common/layouts/base.njk" %}

{% from "govuk/components/button/macro.njk" import govukButton %}
{% from "govuk/components/tag/macro.njk" import govukTag %}
{% from "govuk/components/input/macro.njk" import govukInput %}
{% from "partials/side-nav-icon.njk" import sideNavIcon %}

{#
  Toolbar icons are Material Symbols ligatures (the `material-symbols`
  package, vendored as a font by scripts/update-vendor.js and styled in
  _app-editor-experiment.scss), not inline SVG. The font has no GOV.UK
  equivalent; flagged as a deliberate deviation for this editor chrome.
#}
{% macro ttIcon(name) %}{% set ttGlyphs = {
  "undo": "undo", "redo": "redo", "chevron-down": "expand_more",
  "bullet-list": "format_list_bulleted", "ordered-list": "format_list_numbered",
  "quote": "format_quote", "code-block": "code_blocks", "bold": "format_bold",
  "italic": "format_italic", "strikethrough": "format_strikethrough",
  "code": "code", "underline": "format_underlined", "highlight": "ink_highlighter",
  "link": "link", "superscript": "superscript", "subscript": "subscript",
  "align-left": "format_align_left", "align-center": "format_align_center",
  "align-right": "format_align_right", "align-justify": "format_align_justify",
  "image": "image", "search": "search", "sun": "light_mode", "moon": "dark_mode",
  "comment": "comment", "drag": "drag_indicator"
} %}<span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true">{{ ttGlyphs[name] }}</span>{% endmacro %}

{#
  documentTitle/editorSections (app/routes.js, from ?id=) replace the fixed
  "SFI 23 Guidance document" sample below when a document was actually
  matched — see the {% if editorSections %} branch further down for the
  dynamic content area itself; sections here only needs id/name from each
  one (editorSections also carries heading/body, used by that branch), so
  the sidebar nav loop below works unchanged either way.
#}
{% set pageName = documentTitle if documentTitle else "SFI 23 Guidance document" %}

{% set sections = editorSections if editorSections else [
  { id: "section-1", name: "Overview" },
  { id: "section-2", name: "Eligibility criteria" },
  { id: "section-3", name: "Payment rates" },
  { id: "section-4", name: "Application process" },
  { id: "section-5", name: "Supporting evidence" },
  { id: "section-6", name: "Review dates" }
] %}

{#
  comments (each with a replies array, possibly empty) comes from
  app/routes.js — GET /v5/editor-experiment — rather than being hardcoded
  here: replies added below persist in req.session.data.editorExperimentReplies
  and have to be merged into the base sample thread data server-side
  before this renders. See EDITOR_EXPERIMENT_COMMENTS/
  buildEditorExperimentComments in routes.js. default([]) is just a safety
  net for this one route always passing it.
#}
{% set comments = comments | default([]) %}

{% set checks = [
  { text: "Payment rate figure is outdated", severity: "High", section: "section-3" },
  { text: "Missing alt text on supporting image", severity: "Medium", section: "section-5" },
  { text: "Heading level skips from H2 to H4", severity: "Low", section: "section-4" },
  { text: "Broken internal link to related guidance", severity: "Medium", section: "section-6" },
  { text: "Sentence exceeds recommended reading age", severity: "Low", section: "section-2" }
] %}

{% set severityTags = {
  "High": { text: "High", classes: "govuk-tag--red" },
  "Medium": { text: "Medium", classes: "govuk-tag--yellow" },
  "Low": { text: "Low", classes: "govuk-tag--blue" }
} %}

{% set editorPanel %}
  <form id="guide-panel-toggle-form" method="post" action="{{ panelToggleHref }}" hidden>
    <input type="hidden" name="collapsed" value="{{ 'false' if panelCollapsed else 'true' }}">
    <input type="hidden" name="returnTo" value="{{ returnTo }}">
  </form>

  {% if not panelCollapsed %}
    <div class="app-shell__resize app-shell__resize--aside" role="separator" aria-orientation="vertical"
         aria-label="Resize guide details" aria-controls="guide-panel-body"
         aria-valuemin="{{ panelWidthLimits.min }}" aria-valuemax="{{ panelWidthLimits.max }}"
         data-guide-panel-resize data-width-href="{{ panelWidthHref }}" tabindex="0" hidden></div>
  {% endif %}

  <aside class="app-guide-panel{% if panelCollapsed %} app-guide-panel--collapsed{% endif %}" aria-label="About this guide"{% if panelWidth and not panelCollapsed %} style="--app-guide-panel-width: {{ panelWidth }}px"{% endif %}>
    {% if panelCollapsed %}
      <nav class="app-side-nav app-side-nav--rail" aria-label="About this guide">
        <ul class="app-side-nav__rail-list">
          <li>
            <button type="submit" form="guide-panel-toggle-form" class="app-side-nav__rail-item" data-side-nav-tooltip>
              {{ sideNavIcon("collapse") }}
              <span class="app-side-nav__rail-label">Show guide details</span>
              <span class="app-side-nav__tooltip" aria-hidden="true">Show guide details</span>
            </button>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
    {% else %}
      <div class="app-guide-panel__header">
        <h2 class="govuk-heading-s govuk-!-margin-bottom-0">About this guide</h2>
        <button type="submit" form="guide-panel-toggle-form" class="app-side-nav__icon-button" aria-controls="guide-panel-body">
          {{ sideNavIcon("expand") }}
          <span class="govuk-visually-hidden">Hide guide details</span>
        </button>
      </div>

      <div class="app-guide-panel__body" id="guide-panel-body">
        <div class="app-experiment-actions">
          {{ govukButton({
            text: "Save",
            classes: "govuk-button--secondary govuk-!-width-full govuk-!-margin-bottom-0"
          }) }}
          {{ govukButton({
            text: "Preview",
            type: "button",
            classes: "govuk-button--secondary govuk-!-width-full govuk-!-margin-top-2 govuk-!-margin-bottom-0",
            attributes: { "data-preview-button": true }
          }) }}
          {#
            Consolidation fix: on the old /v5/editor-experiment this button was
            inert (no data attribute, no click handler, no server route) — the
            live crawl behind the consolidation plan found no continuation after
            "Send for approval" in any editor for exactly this reason. V6's own
            editor-2-3-view had already solved this (a review step at
            send-for-approval.njk, then a real Editing→Awaiting approval state
            change) before that page was retired — ported here rather than
            re-invented, same capture-then-navigate pattern as "Preview" below.
          #}
          {{ govukButton({
            text: "Send for approval",
            type: "button",
            classes: "govuk-!-width-full govuk-!-margin-top-2 govuk-!-margin-bottom-0",
            attributes: { "data-send-for-approval-button": true }
          }) }}
          <p class="govuk-hint govuk-!-margin-top-2">
            Once sent for approval, you will not be able to make further changes until it's reviewed.
          </p>
        </div>

        {% if metadata %}
          {% include "playground/guide/partials/metadata-list.njk" %}
        {% endif %}

      <div class="app-experiment-changes app-experiment-changes--panel" data-changes-pane>

        <div class="app-experiment-changes__fixed">

          <h2 class="govuk-heading-s">Review</h2>
          {% if issuesHref %}
            <p class="govuk-body-s"><a class="govuk-link" href="{{ issuesHref }}">View all issues for this guide</a></p>
          {% endif %}

          {#
            Counts in parentheses — comments.length (top-level comments
            only: buildEditorExperimentComments, app/routes.js, already
            merges each thread's replies into its parent rather than
            listing them separately) and checks.length, both server-
            rendered here for the initial count so it is correct before
            any JS runs, same as everything else on this page; updateTabCounts
            (pageScripts) keeps it live afterwards — after a comment is
            deleted, or a draft comment becomes a real one — by recounting
            .app-experiment-comment cards in the DOM rather than tracking a
            separate number, so it can never drift out of sync with what is
            actually showing.
          #}
          <div class="app-experiment-tabs" role="tablist">
            <button type="button" class="app-experiment-tab app-experiment-tab--active" data-tab-button="comments" role="tab" aria-selected="true">Comments ({{ comments.length }})</button>
            <button type="button" class="app-experiment-tab" data-tab-button="checks" role="tab" aria-selected="false">Checks ({{ checks.length }})</button>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div class="app-experiment-changes__scroll">

          {#
            Word-style floating comments: app-experiment-floating-comments
            holds only comments with an anchorId (all three seeded ones —
            see EDITOR_EXPERIMENT_COMMENTS in app/routes.js), rendered in
            normal document flow, in the same top-to-bottom order as their
            anchors. Each app-experiment-comment--floating card gets a
            margin-top from computeFloatingPositions() in pageScripts,
            recalculated on resize and whenever a comment is added/
            deleted/replied to, that roughly lines it up with its anchor's
            current position in the editor pane — see the pageScripts
            comment further down for exactly how, including why this can
            never overlap another card the way an earlier, position:
            absolute version of this could (and once did, for one
            specific comment — see that comment for the incident this
            rebuild fixes).

            Every comment's own thread (its replies list) is always shown
            — there is no collapse/expand step for that — but the reply
            input + Add button underneath stay hidden behind a "Reply"
            link until clicked (data-show-reply-form/data-reply-form, see
            wireCommentReply in pageScripts): an always-open input there
            was tried and reverted, since it felt too heavy with several
            comments on screen at once.
          #}
          <div data-tab-panel="comments">

            <div class="app-experiment-floating-comments" data-comments-list>
              {% for comment in comments %}
                <div class="app-experiment-comment{% if comment.anchorId %} app-experiment-comment--floating{% endif %}"
                     data-comment-id="{{ comment.id }}"
                     data-comment-author="{{ comment.author }}"
                     data-comment-timestamp="{{ comment.timestamp }}"
                     data-comment-text="{{ comment.text }}"
                     data-target-section="{{ comment.section }}"
                     {% if comment.anchorId %}data-anchor-id="{{ comment.anchorId }}"{% endif %}>

                  <div class="app-experiment-comment__header" data-highlight-trigger data-target-section="{{ comment.section }}" tabindex="0" role="button">
                    <div class="app-experiment-comment__row">
                      <p class="govuk-body-s app-experiment-comment__meta">{{ comment.author }} &middot; {{ comment.timestamp }}</p>
                      <button type="button" class="app-experiment-comment__delete" data-delete-comment>Delete</button>
                    </div>
                    <p class="govuk-body app-experiment-comment__text">{{ comment.text }}</p>
                  </div>

                  <div class="app-experiment-comment__thread" data-thread-body>
                    <ul class="app-experiment-comment__replies" data-replies-list>
                      {% for reply in comment.replies %}
                        <li class="app-experiment-comment__reply">
                          <p class="govuk-body-s app-experiment-comment__meta">{{ reply.author }} &middot; {{ reply.timestamp }}</p>
                          <p class="govuk-body app-experiment-comment__text">{{ reply.text }}</p>
                        </li>
                      {% endfor %}
                    </ul>

                    <button type="button" class="app-experiment-comment__reply-link" data-show-reply-form>Reply</button>

                    <div class="app-experiment-reply-form" data-reply-form hidden>
                      <div class="app-experiment-add-comment">
                        {{ govukInput({
                          id: "reply-input-" + comment.id,
                          name: "reply-" + comment.id,
                          label: { text: "Reply", classes: "govuk-visually-hidden" },
                          classes: "govuk-!-margin-bottom-0 app-experiment-add-comment__input",
                          attributes: { "data-reply-input": true }
                        }) }}
                        {{ govukButton({
                          text: "Add",
                          type: "button",
                          classes: "govuk-!-margin-bottom-0 app-experiment-add-comment__button",
                          attributes: { "data-add-reply": true }
                        }) }}
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>
              {% endfor %}
            </div>
            <p class="govuk-hint app-experiment-comments-empty" data-comments-empty{% if comments.length %} hidden{% endif %}>No comments yet. Select text in the guide to add one.</p>

          </div>

          <div data-tab-panel="checks" hidden>

            <ul class="app-experiment-checks">
              {% for check in checks %}
                <li class="app-experiment-check" data-highlight-trigger data-target-section="{{ check.section }}" tabindex="0" role="button">
                  <span class="app-experiment-check__text">{{ check.text }}</span>
                  {{ govukTag(severityTags[check.severity]) }}
                </li>
              {% endfor %}
            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>

      </div>
      </div>
    {% endif %}
  </aside>
{% endset %}

{% block content %}

<div class="app-experiment-page">

<h1 class="govuk-heading-l">{{ pageName }}</h1>

<div class="govuk-grid-row">

  <div class="govuk-grid-column-one-quarter app-experiment-sidebar">

    <h2 class="govuk-heading-s" id="editor-contents-heading">Contents</h2>

    {#
      Each item is a draggable card — reordering is visual/list-only, see
      wireNavDragAndDrop() in pageScripts: it moves the <li> elements
      around within this <ul>, nothing else. It does not touch, and has
      no way to touch, the editor content's own section-N divs or their
      order — dragging "Land checks" above "Force Majeure" here leaves
      the editor content showing them in exactly the order it always
      has. data-highlight-trigger/data-target-section on each link
      (unchanged) still work identically regardless of list position,
      since they look section-N up by id, not by where this link happens
      to sit in the list.
    #}
    <nav aria-labelledby="editor-contents-heading">
      <ul class="app-experiment-nav" data-content-nav-list>
        {% for section in sections %}
          <li class="app-experiment-nav__item{% if section.isSubPart %} app-experiment-nav__item--part{% endif %}{% if loop.first %} app-experiment-nav__item--active{% endif %}">
            <button type="button" class="app-experiment-nav__handle" draggable="true" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">
              {{ ttIcon("drag") }}
            </button>
            <a class="govuk-link app-experiment-nav__link" href="#{{ section.id }}" data-highlight-trigger data-target-section="{{ section.id }}">{{ section.name }}</a>
          </li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </nav>

    {#
      "Add new content": a link, not a button, matching
      .app-experiment-hide-link's existing "looks like a link" reset —
      clicking it hides itself and reveals the input+Add row below
      permanently for the rest of the page's life (same reveal-and-stay
      pattern this page already used for a threaded comment's Reply
      control before that was made always-open — see the Comments panel
      further down), rather than the input hiding itself again after each
      submission, so adding several new headings in a row doesn't need
      re-clicking "Add new content" each time.
    #}
    <button type="button" class="app-experiment-add-content-link" data-show-add-content>Add new content</button>

    <div class="app-experiment-add-content-form" data-add-content-form hidden>
      <div class="app-experiment-add-comment">
        {{ govukInput({
          id: "new-content-heading",
          name: "newContentHeading",
          label: { text: "New content heading", classes: "govuk-visually-hidden" },
          classes: "govuk-!-margin-bottom-0 app-experiment-add-comment__input",
          attributes: { "data-add-content-input": true }
        }) }}
        {{ govukButton({
          text: "Add",
          type: "button",
          classes: "govuk-!-margin-bottom-0 app-experiment-add-comment__button",
          attributes: { "data-add-content-button": true }
        }) }}
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>

  <div class="govuk-grid-column-three-quarters">

    <div class="app-experiment-box" data-experiment-box>

      <div class="app-experiment-editor" data-editor-pane>

        {#
          Tiptap-Simple-Editor-styled toolbar driving the contenteditable
          region with document.execCommand (see wireToolbar in pageScripts).
          Task/lettered lists, code block language, image and search are
          visual only. One tab stop with arrow-key movement (ARIA toolbar);
          keeps the GOV.UK yellow focus ring. A GOV.UK Design System
          deviation: no GDS equivalent exists for an icon toolbar.
        #}
        <div class="tiptap-toolbar" role="toolbar" aria-label="Formatting" aria-controls="editor-content" data-tt-toolbar>

          <div class="tiptap-toolbar__group">
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Undo" title="Undo" data-tt-cmd="undo">{{ ttIcon("undo") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Redo" title="Redo" data-tt-cmd="redo">{{ ttIcon("redo") }}</button>
          </div>

          <div class="tiptap-toolbar__group">
            <div class="tiptap-toolbar__dropdown" data-tt-dropdown>
              <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button tiptap-toolbar__button--wide" title="Text style" aria-label="Text style" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false" data-tt-dropdown-trigger>
                <span data-tt-dropdown-label>Paragraph</span>
                {{ ttIcon("chevron-down") }}
              </button>
              <ul class="tiptap-toolbar__menu" data-tt-dropdown-menu hidden>
                <li><button type="button" data-tt-block="p" data-tt-dropdown-option>Paragraph</button></li>
                <li><button type="button" data-tt-block="h1" data-tt-dropdown-option>Heading 1</button></li>
                <li><button type="button" data-tt-block="h2" data-tt-dropdown-option>Heading 2</button></li>
                <li><button type="button" data-tt-block="h3" data-tt-dropdown-option>Heading 3</button></li>
                <li><button type="button" data-tt-block="h4" data-tt-dropdown-option>Heading 4</button></li>
                <li><button type="button" data-tt-block="h5" data-tt-dropdown-option>Heading 5</button></li>
                <li><button type="button" data-tt-block="h6" data-tt-dropdown-option>Heading 6</button></li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="tiptap-toolbar__group">
            <div class="tiptap-toolbar__dropdown" data-tt-dropdown>
              <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Bullet list" title="Bullet list" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false" data-tt-dropdown-trigger>
                {{ ttIcon("bullet-list") }}
                {{ ttIcon("chevron-down") }}
              </button>
              <ul class="tiptap-toolbar__menu" data-tt-dropdown-menu hidden>
                <li><button type="button" data-tt-cmd="insertUnorderedList" data-tt-dropdown-option>Bullet list</button></li>
                <li><button type="button"  data-tt-dropdown-option>Task list</button></li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="tiptap-toolbar__dropdown" data-tt-dropdown>
              <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Ordered list" title="Ordered list" aria-haspopup="true" aria-expanded="false" data-tt-dropdown-trigger>
                {{ ttIcon("ordered-list") }}
                {{ ttIcon("chevron-down") }}
              </button>
              <ul class="tiptap-toolbar__menu" data-tt-dropdown-menu hidden>
                <li><button type="button" data-tt-cmd="insertOrderedList" data-tt-dropdown-option>Numbered list</button></li>
                <li><button type="button"  data-tt-dropdown-option>Lettered list</button></li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="tiptap-toolbar__group">
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Blockquote" title="Blockquote" data-tt-cmd="blockquote" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("quote") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Code block" title="Code block" data-tt-cmd="pre" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("code-block") }}</button>
          </div>

          <div class="tiptap-toolbar__group">
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Bold" title="Bold" data-tt-cmd="bold" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("bold") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Italic" title="Italic" data-tt-cmd="italic" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("italic") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Strikethrough" title="Strikethrough" data-tt-cmd="strikeThrough" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("strikethrough") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Code" title="Code" data-tt-cmd="code" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("code") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Underline" title="Underline" data-tt-cmd="underline" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("underline") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Highlight" title="Highlight" data-tt-cmd="highlight" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("highlight") }}</button>
          </div>

          <div class="tiptap-toolbar__group">
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Link" title="Link" data-tt-cmd="link" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("link") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Superscript" title="Superscript" data-tt-cmd="superscript" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("superscript") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Subscript" title="Subscript" data-tt-cmd="subscript" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("subscript") }}</button>
          </div>

          <div class="tiptap-toolbar__group">
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Align left" title="Align left" data-tt-cmd="justifyLeft" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("align-left") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Align center" title="Align center" data-tt-cmd="justifyCenter" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("align-center") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Align right" title="Align right" data-tt-cmd="justifyRight" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("align-right") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Justify" title="Justify" data-tt-cmd="justifyFull" aria-pressed="false">{{ ttIcon("align-justify") }}</button>
          </div>

          <div class="tiptap-toolbar__group">
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button tiptap-toolbar__button--wide" aria-label="Add image" title="Add image">
              {{ ttIcon("image") }}
              <span>Add</span>
            </button>
          </div>

          <div class="tiptap-toolbar__spacer"></div>

          <div class="tiptap-toolbar__group">
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" aria-label="Search" title="Search">{{ ttIcon("search") }}</button>
            <button type="button" class="tiptap-toolbar__button" data-tt-theme-toggle aria-label="Toggle dark mode" title="Toggle dark mode" aria-pressed="false">
              <span data-tt-theme-icon>{{ ttIcon("sun") }}</span>
            </button>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div class="app-experiment-content" id="editor-content" contenteditable="true" data-editor-content>

          {% if restoredEditorHtml %}
            {#
              Set by routes.js only when arriving back from "Back to editor"
              on saved-document-view.html's preview flow, and only when the
              stored preview's id still matches this page's own ?id= (or
              lack of one) — see /v5/editor-experiment there. This is an
              exact copy of [data-editor-content]'s own innerHTML captured
              client-side just before Preview navigated away (its "html"
              field — see the Preview button's click handler in pageScripts
              below), so it already contains the same section-N ids,
              app-editor-anchor spans and app-experiment-boundary markers
              as whichever of the two branches below would otherwise have
              rendered — every existing pageScripts selector keeps working
              against it unchanged.
            #}
            {{ restoredEditorHtml | safe }}
          {% elif editorSections %}
            {#
              One section per editorSections entry (app/routes.js) —
              already flattened to whichever of the three content shapes
              this document actually has (flat steps, nested sections>parts
              — one per part, not per top-level section — or the generic
              3-phase placeholder), so this loop does not need to know
              which one it is looking at. Body rendering matches
              saved-document-view.html's own: a string is a paragraph
              (through renderLinkMarkers, so any {{LINK:...}} marker
              becomes the same non-navigating placeholder link it does
              there), an array is a bullet list, and the literal string
              "CASE_NOTE_BLOCK" — present in exactly one flat-steps
              document's last step — renders nothing here rather than that
              page's fixed case-note markup, since there is nothing
              editor-specific to show in its place. Each section gets the
              same app-experiment-boundary marker as the fixed sample
              content below — see the comment on that block there.
            #}
            {% for section in editorSections %}
              <div class="app-experiment-boundary" data-boundary>
                <span class="app-experiment-boundary__label">Start of {{ section.name }}</span>
                <div class="app-experiment-boundary__line"></div>
                <button type="button" class="app-experiment-boundary__handle" data-boundary-handle aria-label="Drag, or use the up and down arrow keys, to move paragraphs between this section and the one before it" tabindex="0"></button>
              </div>
              <div id="{{ section.id }}">
                <{{ "h3" if section.isSubPart else "h2" }} class="govuk-heading-{{ "s" if section.isSubPart else "m" }}">
                  {{ section.heading }}
                </{{ "h3" if section.isSubPart else "h2" }}>
                {% if section.html %}
                  {{ section.html | safe }}
                {% endif %}
                {% for entry in section.body %}
                  {% if entry == "CASE_NOTE_BLOCK" %}
                  {% elif entry is string %}
                    <p class="govuk-body">{{ entry | renderLinkMarkers }}</p>
                  {% else %}
                    <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
                      {% for item in entry %}
                        <li>{{ item | renderLinkMarkers }}</li>
                      {% endfor %}
                    </ul>
                  {% endif %}
                {% endfor %}
              </div>
            {% endfor %}
          {% else %}

            {#
              app-editor-anchor spans below are the fixed sample's only
              inline anchors — hand-placed to match
              EDITOR_EXPERIMENT_COMMENTS' anchorId fields (app/routes.js)
              exactly, since there is nowhere generic to derive "the text
              a comment refers to" from for a hardcoded sample like this
              one. A real document loaded via ?id= (the branch above) has
              no equivalent spans — comments still show, they just have
              nothing to scroll to on an icon click there.

              app-experiment-boundary markers (one per section) are the
              second thing hand-added here — see wireBoundaryHandle in
              pageScripts for what dragging (or arrow-keying) its handle
              actually does: reassigns which of the two adjacent
              section-N divs each paragraph either side of it belongs to,
              snapped to paragraph boundaries.
            #}
            <div class="app-experiment-boundary" data-boundary>
              <span class="app-experiment-boundary__label">Start of Overview</span>
              <div class="app-experiment-boundary__line"></div>
              <button type="button" class="app-experiment-boundary__handle" data-boundary-handle aria-label="Drag, or use the up and down arrow keys, to move paragraphs between this section and the one before it" tabindex="0"></button>
            </div>
            <div id="section-1">
              <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Overview</h2>
              <p class="govuk-body">This guidance explains the Sustainable Farming Incentive (SFI) 2023 offer, including who can apply, what actions are available, and how payments are calculated.</p>
              <p class="govuk-body">It's aimed at farmers who manage land in England and want to understand how the scheme fits alongside other funding they may already receive.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="app-experiment-boundary" data-boundary>
              <span class="app-experiment-boundary__label">Start of Eligibility criteria</span>
              <div class="app-experiment-boundary__line"></div>
              <button type="button" class="app-experiment-boundary__handle" data-boundary-handle aria-label="Drag, or use the up and down arrow keys, to move paragraphs between this section and the one before it" tabindex="0"></button>
            </div>
            <div id="section-2">
              <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Eligibility criteria</h2>
              <h3 class="govuk-heading-s">Land eligibility</h3>
              <p class="govuk-body">You can apply if you have management control of the land and it is registered on the Rural Payments service.</p>
              <h3 class="govuk-heading-s">Business eligibility</h3>
              <p class="govuk-body">Applicants must have a valid Single Business Identifier and be actively farming the land included in the application.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="app-experiment-boundary" data-boundary>
              <span class="app-experiment-boundary__label">Start of Payment rates</span>
              <div class="app-experiment-boundary__line"></div>
              <button type="button" class="app-experiment-boundary__handle" data-boundary-handle aria-label="Drag, or use the up and down arrow keys, to move paragraphs between this section and the one before it" tabindex="0"></button>
            </div>
            <div id="section-3">
              <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Payment rates</h2>
              <p class="govuk-body">Payment rates are published annually and reviewed each scheme year. <span class="text-highlight-active">The current baseline payment is £22 per hectare for the standard soil management action.</span></p>
              <p class="govuk-body">Rates can vary depending on which actions are combined and the total area entered into the scheme.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="app-experiment-boundary" data-boundary>
              <span class="app-experiment-boundary__label">Start of Application process</span>
              <div class="app-experiment-boundary__line"></div>
              <button type="button" class="app-experiment-boundary__handle" data-boundary-handle aria-label="Drag, or use the up and down arrow keys, to move paragraphs between this section and the one before it" tabindex="0"></button>
            </div>
            <div id="section-4">
              <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Application process</h2>
              <p class="govuk-body">Applications are submitted through the Rural Payments service and assessed in the order they are received.</p>
              <p class="govuk-body">You'll need to confirm your land parcels and select the actions you want to include before submitting.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="app-experiment-boundary" data-boundary>
              <span class="app-experiment-boundary__label">Start of Supporting evidence</span>
              <div class="app-experiment-boundary__line"></div>
              <button type="button" class="app-experiment-boundary__handle" data-boundary-handle aria-label="Drag, or use the up and down arrow keys, to move paragraphs between this section and the one before it" tabindex="0"></button>
            </div>
            <div id="section-5">
              <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Supporting evidence</h2>
              <p class="govuk-body">Keep records of any actions carried out, including dates and photographs where relevant.</p>
              <p class="govuk-body">Evidence should be retained for the duration of the agreement in case it is requested during an inspection.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="app-experiment-boundary" data-boundary>
              <span class="app-experiment-boundary__label">Start of Review dates</span>
              <div class="app-experiment-boundary__line"></div>
              <button type="button" class="app-experiment-boundary__handle" data-boundary-handle aria-label="Drag, or use the up and down arrow keys, to move paragraphs between this section and the one before it" tabindex="0"></button>
            </div>
            <div id="section-6">
              <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Review dates</h2>
              <p class="govuk-body">This guidance is reviewed annually alongside the wider scheme update.</p>
              <p class="govuk-body">The next scheduled review is due in the spring, ahead of the following scheme year's application window.</p>
            </div>

          {% endif %}

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>

  </div>

</div>

</div>

{% if not sideNav %}{{ editorPanel | safe }}{% endif %}

{% endblock %}

{% block shellAside %}{% if sideNav %}{{ editorPanel | safe }}{% endif %}{% endblock %}

{% block pageScripts %}
  {#
    {anchorId, sectionId, selectedText} for every comment created live via
    the floating "add comment" icon and still around (app/routes.js —
    buildAddedCommentAnchors) — restoreAddedCommentAnchors() below reads
    this once, on load, to re-wrap each selectedText inside section
    sectionId in a fresh app-editor-anchor span before floatingCards is
    built, since the card itself is already server-rendered (via the
    ordinary comments loop above) but its anchor span in the editor
    content is not — nothing server-side injects HTML into the editor
    content, only this.
  #}
  <script type="application/json" data-added-comment-anchors>{{ addedCommentAnchorsJson | default("[]") | safe }}</script>
  <script>
    (function () {
      var editorContent = document.querySelector('[data-editor-content]')

      // Formatting toolbar, driven by document.execCommand on the
      // contenteditable region (prototype-grade: nothing is saved).
      // Buttons keep the editor selection by cancelling mousedown;
      // aria-pressed and the style dropdown label follow the caret on
      // selectionchange. The toolbar is one tab stop (roving tabindex).
      var BLOCK_CLASSES = {
        P: 'govuk-body', H1: 'govuk-heading-l', H2: 'govuk-heading-m',
        H3: 'govuk-heading-s', H4: 'govuk-heading-s', H5: 'govuk-heading-s',
        H6: 'govuk-heading-s', BLOCKQUOTE: 'govuk-inset-text',
        UL: 'govuk-list govuk-list--bullet', OL: 'govuk-list govuk-list--number'
      }
      var BLOCK_LABELS = {
        P: 'Paragraph', H1: 'Heading 1', H2: 'Heading 2', H3: 'Heading 3',
        H4: 'Heading 4', H5: 'Heading 5', H6: 'Heading 6'
      }
      var HIGHLIGHT = 'rgb(255, 221, 0)'

      // Blocks created by execCommand have no GOV.UK class; add one.
      function styleNewBlocks () {
        var tags = Object.keys(BLOCK_CLASSES).join(',').toLowerCase()
        editorContent.querySelectorAll(tags).forEach(function (el) {
          if (!el.getAttribute('class')) el.className = BLOCK_CLASSES[el.tagName]
        })
      }

      function selectionInEditor () {
        var sel = window.getSelection()
        return sel && sel.rangeCount && editorContent.contains(sel.anchorNode)
      }

      function closestInEditor (selector) {
        if (!selectionInEditor()) return null
        var node = window.getSelection().anchorNode
        var el = node.nodeType === 1 ? node : node.parentElement
        var found = el && el.closest(selector)
        return found && editorContent.contains(found) ? found : null
      }

      function currentBlock () {
        var el = closestInEditor('p,h1,h2,h3,h4,h5,h6,blockquote,pre,li')
        return el ? el.tagName : null
      }

      function toggleBlock (tag) {
        document.execCommand('formatBlock', false, currentBlock() === tag.toUpperCase() ? 'p' : tag)
      }

      function toggleCode () {
        var code = closestInEditor('code')
        var sel = window.getSelection()
        if (code) {
          code.replaceWith(document.createTextNode(code.textContent))
        } else if (!sel.isCollapsed) {
          var html = document.createElement('div')
          html.appendChild(sel.getRangeAt(0).cloneContents())
          document.execCommand('insertHTML', false, '<code>' + html.textContent.replace(/</g, '&lt;') + '</code>')
        }
      }

      function toggleLink () {
        if (closestInEditor('a')) {
          document.execCommand('unlink')
          return
        }
        var url = window.prompt('Link address', 'https://')
        if (url && url !== 'https://') document.execCommand('createLink', false, url)
      }

      function toggleHighlight () {
        document.execCommand('styleWithCSS', false, true)
        var on = document.queryCommandValue('hiliteColor') === HIGHLIGHT
        document.execCommand('hiliteColor', false, on ? 'transparent' : HIGHLIGHT)
        document.execCommand('styleWithCSS', false, false)
      }

      function runCommand (cmd) {
        if (!selectionInEditor()) editorContent.focus()
        if (cmd === 'blockquote' || cmd === 'pre') toggleBlock(cmd)
        else if (cmd === 'code') toggleCode()
        else if (cmd === 'link') toggleLink()
        else if (cmd === 'highlight') toggleHighlight()
        else document.execCommand(cmd)
        styleNewBlocks()
      }

      function commandActive (cmd) {
        if (!selectionInEditor()) return false
        if (cmd === 'blockquote' || cmd === 'pre') return currentBlock() === cmd.toUpperCase()
        if (cmd === 'code') return !!closestInEditor('code')
        if (cmd === 'link') return !!closestInEditor('a')
        if (cmd === 'highlight') return document.queryCommandValue('hiliteColor') === HIGHLIGHT
        try { return document.queryCommandState(cmd) } catch (e) { return false }
      }

      function wireToolbar () {
        var toolbar = document.querySelector('[data-tt-toolbar]')
        if (!toolbar || !editorContent) return

        var dropdowns = toolbar.querySelectorAll('[data-tt-dropdown]')
        var styleLabel = toolbar.querySelector('[data-tt-dropdown-label]')
        var stateButtons = toolbar.querySelectorAll('[aria-pressed][data-tt-cmd]')

        // Keep the editor selection when a toolbar button is pressed.
        toolbar.addEventListener('mousedown', function (event) {
          if (event.target.closest('button')) event.preventDefault()
        })

        function closeAllDropdowns (except) {
          dropdowns.forEach(function (dropdown) {
            if (dropdown === except) return
            dropdown.querySelector('[data-tt-dropdown-trigger]').setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false')
            dropdown.querySelector('[data-tt-dropdown-menu]').hidden = true
          })
        }

        function syncState () {
          stateButtons.forEach(function (button) {
            button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(commandActive(button.getAttribute('data-tt-cmd'))))
          })
          if (styleLabel && selectionInEditor()) {
            styleLabel.textContent = BLOCK_LABELS[currentBlock()] || 'Paragraph'
          }
        }

        toolbar.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
          var option = event.target.closest('[data-tt-dropdown-option]')
          var button = event.target.closest('button')
          if (!button) return
          var cmd = button.getAttribute('data-tt-cmd')
          var block = button.getAttribute('data-tt-block')

          if (button.hasAttribute('data-tt-dropdown-trigger')) {
            var dropdown = button.closest('[data-tt-dropdown]')
            var menu = dropdown.querySelector('[data-tt-dropdown-menu]')
            var opening = menu.hidden
            closeAllDropdowns(dropdown)
            menu.hidden = !opening
            button.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(opening))
            if (opening) {
              var first = menu.querySelector('button')
              if (first) first.focus()
            }
            return
          }

          if (block) {
            if (!selectionInEditor()) editorContent.focus()
            document.execCommand('formatBlock', false, block)
            styleNewBlocks()
          } else if (cmd) {
            runCommand(cmd)
          }

          if (option) {
            var owner = option.closest('[data-tt-dropdown]')
            closeAllDropdowns(null)
            owner.querySelector('[data-tt-dropdown-trigger]').focus()
          }
          syncState()
        })

        document.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
          if (!event.target.closest('[data-tt-dropdown]')) closeAllDropdowns(null)
        })
        document.addEventListener('selectionchange', syncState)

        // Roving tabindex: one tab stop, arrows/Home/End move within the bar.
        var items = Array.prototype.slice.call(
          toolbar.querySelectorAll('.tiptap-toolbar__button')
        ).filter(function (item) { return !item.closest('[data-tt-dropdown-menu]') })
        function setActive (item) {
          items.forEach(function (candidate) { candidate.tabIndex = candidate === item ? 0 : -1 })
        }
        setActive(items[0])
        items.forEach(function (item) {
          item.addEventListener('focus', function () { setActive(item) })
        })
        toolbar.addEventListener('keydown', function (event) {
          var inMenu = event.target.closest('[data-tt-dropdown-menu]')
          if (event.key === 'Escape') {
            var open = event.target.closest('[data-tt-dropdown]')
            closeAllDropdowns(null)
            if (open) open.querySelector('[data-tt-dropdown-trigger]').focus()
            return
          }
          if (inMenu) {
            var options = Array.prototype.slice.call(inMenu.querySelectorAll('button'))
            var at = options.indexOf(event.target)
            if (event.key === 'ArrowDown') options[(at + 1) % options.length].focus()
            else if (event.key === 'ArrowUp') options[(at - 1 + options.length) % options.length].focus()
            else return
            event.preventDefault()
            return
          }
          var index = items.indexOf(event.target)
          if (index === -1) return
          var next
          if (event.key === 'ArrowRight') next = items[(index + 1) % items.length]
          else if (event.key === 'ArrowLeft') next = items[(index - 1 + items.length) % items.length]
          else if (event.key === 'Home') next = items[0]
          else if (event.key === 'End') next = items[items.length - 1]
          else return
          event.preventDefault()
          next.focus()
        })

        // Visual only: the dark-mode toggle swaps its own icon and state.
        var themeToggle = toolbar.querySelector('[data-tt-theme-toggle]')
        if (themeToggle) {
          themeToggle.addEventListener('click', function () {
            var pressed = themeToggle.getAttribute('aria-pressed') === 'true'
            themeToggle.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(!pressed))
            themeToggle.querySelector('.material-symbols-outlined').textContent = pressed ? 'light_mode' : 'dark_mode'
          })
        }
      }

      wireToolbar()

      // Comments / Checks tabs.
      var tabButtons = document.querySelectorAll('[data-tab-button]')
      tabButtons.forEach(function (tabButton) {
        tabButton.addEventListener('click', function () {
          tabButtons.forEach(function (button) {
            var active = button === tabButton
            button.classList.toggle('app-experiment-tab--active', active)
            button.setAttribute('aria-selected', String(active))

            var panel = document.querySelector('[data-tab-panel="' + button.dataset.tabButton + '"]')
            if (panel) panel.hidden = !active
          })
        })
      })

      // Keeps each tab's "(n)" count (server-rendered initially from
      // comments.length/checks.length above, so it is correct before any
      // JS runs) in sync after something changes it: a comment is deleted
      // (wireCommentDelete) or a draft comment becomes a real one
      // (wireDraftCommentCard). Recounts .app-experiment-comment cards in
      // the DOM each time rather than tracking a running number
      // separately, so it can never drift out of sync with what is
      // actually showing; an in-progress (not yet submitted)
      // .app-experiment-comment--floating draft — still just an
      // input+Add button, no [data-draft-input] once submitted — is
      // deliberately excluded, since it is not yet a comment. Checks
      // never change after load (no add/delete UI for them), so this
      // only needs calling for the Comments side in practice, but both
      // are recomputed together for simplicity.
      var commentsTabButton = document.querySelector('[data-tab-button="comments"]')
      var checksTabButton = document.querySelector('[data-tab-button="checks"]')

      function updateTabCounts () {
        if (commentsTabButton) {
          var commentCount = Array.prototype.filter.call(
            document.querySelectorAll('.app-experiment-comment'),
            function (card) { return !card.querySelector('[data-draft-input]') }
          ).length
          commentsTabButton.textContent = 'Comments (' + commentCount + ')'
          var emptyHint = document.querySelector('[data-comments-empty]')
          if (emptyHint) {
            emptyHint.hidden = document.querySelectorAll('.app-experiment-comment').length > 0
          }
        }
        if (checksTabButton) {
          checksTabButton.textContent = 'Checks (' + document.querySelectorAll('.app-experiment-check').length + ')'
        }
      }

      // Flash-and-fade highlight, shared by every "clicking X highlights Y"
      // interaction on this page (Content nav links, comment/check
      // triggers below, and the anchor-icon click further down) — briefly
      // confirms "this is what you clicked", then fades back to normal
      // rather than staying highlighted indefinitely. Clears any highlight
      // already in progress first (including its own pending fade-out
      // timeout, so a rapid second click doesn't leave an orphaned timer
      // that clears the wrong, newer highlight early) so there is still
      // only ever one flash active at a time, same as before. Any number
      // of elements can be flashed together as one single flash (e.g. a
      // comment's own section AND its card at once) so they read as a
      // single moment rather than two separately-timed ones.
      var FLASH_HOLD_MS = 1500
      var activeFlashTimeout = null

      function flashHighlight () {
        var targets = Array.prototype.filter.call(arguments, Boolean)
        if (!targets.length) return

        window.clearTimeout(activeFlashTimeout)
        document.querySelectorAll('.text-highlight-active').forEach(function (el) {
          el.classList.remove('text-highlight-active')
        })

        targets.forEach(function (el) { el.classList.add('text-highlight-active') })
        activeFlashTimeout = window.setTimeout(function () {
          targets.forEach(function (el) { el.classList.remove('text-highlight-active') })
        }, FLASH_HOLD_MS)
      }

      // Clicking a Content nav link, a comment or a check flashes the
      // section it is linked to and scrolls it into view. Every comment
      // card always has a real data-target-section now (its own anchor's
      // section, whichever of the three sources built it — see
      // wrapRangeInAnchor below), but the "look it up, do nothing if it's
      // not there" fallback stays regardless, same as a target id that
      // doesn't resolve to anything for any other reason. A trigger that
      // sits inside its own comment card (a comment's header — checks and
      // Content nav links don't) flashes that card too, alongside its
      // section, so both read as confirmed together — see the
      // .app-experiment-comment styling in _app-editor-experiment.scss for
      // why it, not just editor text, can carry this highlight.
      // event.preventDefault() matters specifically for a Content nav
      // link (a real <a href="#section-id">, unlike the div/li role="button"
      // triggers): without it, the browser's own instant anchor-jump would
      // fire alongside this smooth one and the two would fight.
      //
      // Pulled out into its own function, rather than one querySelectorAll
      // + forEach run once at page load, since a comment added later needs
      // exactly the same click/keydown behaviour wired up as the fixed
      // sample ones already have.
      function wireHighlightTrigger (trigger) {
        trigger.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
          var target = document.getElementById(trigger.dataset.targetSection)
          if (!target) return

          event.preventDefault()
          flashHighlight(target, trigger.closest('.app-experiment-comment'))
          target.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' })
        })

        // role="button" on a <div>/<li> needs its own keyboard activation —
        // a real <button> (or, for a Content nav link, <a>) would get this
        // for free, but this is harmless to also attach there.
        trigger.addEventListener('keydown', function (event) {
          if (event.key !== 'Enter' && event.key !== ' ') return
          event.preventDefault()
          trigger.click()
        })
      }

      document.querySelectorAll('[data-highlight-trigger]').forEach(wireHighlightTrigger)

      function escapeHtml (value) {
        return String(value)
          .replace(/&/g, '&amp;')
          .replace(/</g, '&lt;')
          .replace(/>/g, '&gt;')
          .replace(/"/g, '&quot;')
          .replace(/'/g, '&#39;')
      }

      // The SVG used for every app-editor-anchor__icon, whether hand-
      // written into the fixed sample's own markup, or (both cases
      // below) built here in JS — identical either way, so a comment
      // created live looks no different from one that was always there.
      var ANCHOR_ICON_SVG = '{{ ttIcon("comment") }}'

      function buildAnchorIconHTML (anchorId, author) {
        return '<button type="button" class="app-editor-anchor__icon" data-anchor-icon="' + anchorId + '" aria-label="View ' + escapeHtml(author) + '’s comment">' + ANCHOR_ICON_SVG + '</button>'
      }

      // Wraps range in a new app-editor-anchor span + trailing icon —
      // the exact same pair the fixed sample's own three anchors already
      // are (see that markup) and EDITOR_HEADING_ANCHORS wraps a real
      // document's section heading in (app/routes.js) — this is the
      // third source of the same two elements, not a parallel mechanism.
      // Returns the new span, or null if range can't be wrapped as a
      // single element (surroundContents throws if it crosses element
      // boundaries, e.g. a selection spanning two paragraphs) — same
      // failure mode and same "just don't create it" handling this
      // page's very first selection-wrapping code already established.
      function wrapRangeInAnchor (range, anchorId, author) {
        var span = document.createElement('span')
        span.className = 'app-editor-anchor'
        span.setAttribute('data-anchor-id', anchorId)

        try {
          range.surroundContents(span)
        } catch (error) {
          return null
        }

        span.insertAdjacentHTML('afterend', ' ' + buildAnchorIconHTML(anchorId, author))
        return span
      }

      // Finds the first occurrence of text inside section sectionId and
      // returns a Range spanning it, or null if that section or that
      // exact text isn't there (editor content changed since, or the
      // text happened to cross more than one text node — same
      // constraint wrapRangeInAnchor's own surroundContents already
      // has). Used only to restore an anchor already created in an
      // earlier visit (restoreAddedCommentAnchors below) — a live
      // selection never needs this, it already has a real Range from
      // window.getSelection() directly.
      function findTextRangeInSection (sectionId, text) {
        var section = sectionId && document.getElementById(sectionId)
        if (!section || !text) return null

        var walker = document.createTreeWalker(section, NodeFilter.SHOW_TEXT)
        var node
        while ((node = walker.nextNode())) {
          var index = node.nodeValue.indexOf(text)
          if (index !== -1) {
            var range = document.createRange()
            range.setStart(node, index)
            range.setEnd(node, index + text.length)
            return range
          }
        }
        return null
      }

      // Re-wraps every comment created live in an earlier visit (still
      // around after Delete/session expiry — see
      // buildAddedCommentAnchors, app/routes.js) inside a fresh
      // app-editor-anchor span, before floatingCards (further down) scans
      // the page for anchors to pair comment cards against — the card
      // itself is already server-rendered (comments loop above, same as
      // any other comment), only its anchor span in the editor content
      // needs restoring here. A comment whose text isn't found any more
      // (e.g. a real document's content changed) is simply left without
      // a working anchor — its card still shows, just not floating,
      // rather than this throwing.
      function restoreAddedCommentAnchors () {
        var dataScript = document.querySelector('[data-added-comment-anchors]')
        if (!dataScript) return

        var anchors
        try {
          anchors = JSON.parse(dataScript.textContent)
        } catch (error) {
          return
        }

        anchors.forEach(function (entry) {
          var card = document.querySelector('.app-experiment-comment[data-anchor-id="' + entry.anchorId + '"]')
          var author = card ? card.dataset.commentAuthor : ''
          var range = findTextRangeInSection(entry.sectionId, entry.selectedText)
          if (range) wrapRangeInAnchor(range, entry.anchorId, author)
        })
      }

      restoreAddedCommentAnchors()

      // Google Docs-style "select text -> floating icon -> new anchored
      // comment". selectionTrigger is one reusable element (position:
      // fixed, viewport coordinates) shown next to whatever was most
      // recently selected, rather than a separate one per possible
      // selection.
      //
      // pendingSelectionRange holds a *clone* of the Range at the moment
      // the mouse was released — cloned, not the live Selection itself,
      // since clicking the trigger (outside the text that was selected)
      // collapses the browser's actual selection before that click
      // handler runs, same reason the very first version of this feature
      // on this page had to do the same thing.
      var editorSelectionTrigger = document.createElement('button')
      editorSelectionTrigger.type = 'button'
      editorSelectionTrigger.className = 'app-editor-selection-trigger'
      editorSelectionTrigger.setAttribute('aria-label', 'Add comment')
      editorSelectionTrigger.hidden = true
      editorSelectionTrigger.innerHTML = ANCHOR_ICON_SVG
      document.body.appendChild(editorSelectionTrigger)

      var pendingSelectionRange = null
      var addedCommentCounter = 0

      function hideSelectionTrigger () {
        editorSelectionTrigger.hidden = true
        pendingSelectionRange = null
      }

      if (editorContent) {
        editorContent.addEventListener('mouseup', function () {
          // A tick after mouseup, not on it directly — the browser hasn't
          // always finished updating window.getSelection() by the time
          // this event itself fires.
          window.setTimeout(function () {
            var selection = window.getSelection()
            if (!selection || selection.isCollapsed || !selection.anchorNode || !selection.focusNode ||
                !editorContent.contains(selection.anchorNode) || !editorContent.contains(selection.focusNode)) {
              hideSelectionTrigger()
              return
            }

            var text = selection.toString().trim()
            if (!text) {
              hideSelectionTrigger()
              return
            }

            pendingSelectionRange = selection.getRangeAt(0).cloneRange()
            var rect = pendingSelectionRange.getBoundingClientRect()
            editorSelectionTrigger.style.top = (window.scrollY + rect.top - 34) + 'px'
            editorSelectionTrigger.style.left = (window.scrollX + Math.max(rect.right - 28, rect.left)) + 'px'
            editorSelectionTrigger.hidden = false
          }, 0)
        })
      }

      // Disappears on any click that isn't the trigger itself and isn't
      // the start of a fresh selection inside the editor (which would
      // just show it again, freshly positioned, on that drag's own
      // mouseup) — covers both "clicked away" and "selection cleared by
      // clicking", the two cases asked for.
      document.addEventListener('mousedown', function (event) {
        if (event.target === editorSelectionTrigger || editorSelectionTrigger.contains(event.target)) return
        if (editorContent && editorContent.contains(event.target)) return
        hideSelectionTrigger()
      })

      // The reply/comments panel body every card's own header +
      // (empty, until a real reply exists) replies list + Delete would
      // normally have — built as one HTML string here since the exact
      // same shape is needed for both a brand new comment (below) and,
      // previously, one added through the box this feature replaced.
      function buildCommentCardInnerHTML (id, author, timestamp, text, sectionId) {
        return (
          '<div class="app-experiment-comment__header" data-highlight-trigger data-target-section="' + escapeHtml(sectionId || '') + '" tabindex="0" role="button">' +
            '<div class="app-experiment-comment__row">' +
              '<p class="govuk-body-s app-experiment-comment__meta">' + escapeHtml(author) + ' &middot; ' + escapeHtml(timestamp) + '</p>' +
              '<button type="button" class="app-experiment-comment__delete" data-delete-comment>Delete</button>' +
            '</div>' +
            '<p class="govuk-body app-experiment-comment__text">' + escapeHtml(text) + '</p>' +
          '</div>' +
          '<div class="app-experiment-comment__thread" data-thread-body>' +
            '<ul class="app-experiment-comment__replies" data-replies-list></ul>' +
            '<button type="button" class="app-experiment-comment__reply-link" data-show-reply-form>Reply</button>' +
            '<div class="app-experiment-reply-form" data-reply-form hidden>' +
              '<div class="app-experiment-add-comment">' +
                '<div class="govuk-form-group govuk-!-margin-bottom-0 app-experiment-add-comment__input">' +
                  '<label class="govuk-label govuk-visually-hidden" for="reply-input-' + id + '">Reply</label>' +
                  '<input class="govuk-input" id="reply-input-' + id + '" name="reply-' + id + '" type="text" data-reply-input>' +
                '</div>' +
                '<button type="button" class="govuk-button govuk-!-margin-bottom-0 app-experiment-add-comment__button" data-add-reply>Add</button>' +
              '</div>' +
            '</div>' +
          '</div>'
        )
      }

      // What a brand new anchored card looks like before it has any text
      // of its own — just an input + Add in the same padded slot the
      // header will occupy once submitted (app-experiment-comment__header
      // reused purely for its padding, not its usual meta/text content),
      // so it takes up roughly the same footprint computeFloatingPositions
      // will be pushing other cards clear of either way.
      function buildDraftCommentCardHTML (id) {
        return (
          '<div class="app-experiment-comment__header">' +
            '<div class="app-experiment-add-comment">' +
              '<div class="govuk-form-group govuk-!-margin-bottom-0 app-experiment-add-comment__input">' +
                '<label class="govuk-label govuk-visually-hidden" for="draft-input-' + id + '">Comment</label>' +
                '<input class="govuk-input" id="draft-input-' + id + '" name="draft-' + id + '" type="text" data-draft-input>' +
              '</div>' +
              '<button type="button" class="govuk-button govuk-!-margin-bottom-0 app-experiment-add-comment__button" data-draft-add>Add</button>' +
            '</div>' +
          '</div>'
        )
      }

      // Submitting the draft turns it into a real card in place (same
      // element, new innerHTML) rather than replacing it with a
      // different one, so it keeps its spot in floatingCards without
      // needing to re-pair it with its anchor span. wireCommentCard,
      // wireHighlightTrigger and scheduleFloatingRecalc are all reused
      // completely unchanged — a card built this way is functionally
      // identical to one the server rendered, Reply/Delete included.
      function wireDraftCommentCard (card, meta) {
        var input = card.querySelector('[data-draft-input]')
        var addButton = card.querySelector('[data-draft-add]')
        if (!input || !addButton) return

        addButton.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
          event.stopPropagation()
          var text = input.value.trim()
          if (!text) return

          card.innerHTML = buildCommentCardInnerHTML(card.dataset.commentId, 'You', 'Just now', text, meta.sectionId)
          card.setAttribute('data-comment-author', 'You')
          card.setAttribute('data-comment-timestamp', 'Just now')
          card.setAttribute('data-comment-text', text)
          card.setAttribute('data-target-section', meta.sectionId || '')

          wireCommentCard(card)
          wireHighlightTrigger(card.querySelector('[data-highlight-trigger]'))
          scheduleFloatingRecalc()
          updateTabCounts()

          if (window.fetch) {
            fetch('/playground/editor/add-comment', {
              method: 'POST',
              headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
              body: JSON.stringify({
                commentId: card.dataset.commentId,
                anchorId: meta.anchorId,
                sectionId: meta.sectionId,
                text: text,
                selectedText: meta.selectedText
              })
            }).catch(function () {})
          }
        })
      }

      editorSelectionTrigger.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
        event.stopPropagation()
        if (!pendingSelectionRange) return

        var range = pendingSelectionRange
        hideSelectionTrigger()

        addedCommentCounter += 1
        var anchorId = 'anchor-live-' + addedCommentCounter
        var commentId = 'live-comment-' + addedCommentCounter

        var span = wrapRangeInAnchor(range, anchorId, 'You')
        if (!span) return

        var sectionEl = span.closest('[id^="section-"]')
        var sectionId = sectionEl ? sectionEl.id : ''
        var selectedText = span.textContent

        var wrapper = document.createElement('div')
        wrapper.innerHTML =
          '<div class="app-experiment-comment app-experiment-comment--floating" data-comment-id="' + commentId + '" data-anchor-id="' + anchorId + '" data-target-section="' + escapeHtml(sectionId) + '">' +
            buildDraftCommentCardHTML(commentId) +
          '</div>'
        var card = wrapper.firstElementChild

        insertFloatingCard(card, span)
        wireDraftCommentCard(card, { anchorId: anchorId, sectionId: sectionId, selectedText: selectedText })
        scheduleFloatingRecalc()

        var draftInput = card.querySelector('[data-draft-input]')
        if (draftInput) draftInput.focus()
      })

      // Comment cards — server-rendered or turned from a draft into a
      // real one live (wireDraftCommentCard above) — share one wiring
      // pass: reply ("Reply" reveals the input; submitting appends to
      // the card's own replies list) and delete. data-highlight-trigger
      // is deliberately NOT wired in
      // here — it is covered separately by the
      // document.querySelectorAll('[data-highlight-trigger]') pass above
      // for every card already on the page at load, and explicitly by
      // name for each one added afterwards (wireDraftCommentCard above),
      // so it is never bound twice on the same header.
      function wireCommentCard (card) {
        wireCommentReply(card)
        wireCommentDelete(card)
      }

      // "Reply" is a hyperlink-style button that reveals the input + Add
      // button beneath it on click, then hides itself — an earlier
      // version had that input+button always open instead, which turned
      // out to feel too heavy with several comments on screen at once,
      // so this reverts to the reveal-on-click version. Submitting
      // appends the reply to the card's own data-replies-list (reusing
      // escapeHtml() from above) and fires a background POST to persist
      // it into req.session.data.editorExperimentReplies (app/routes.js),
      // so it is still there after a reload — same fire-and-forget
      // pattern as "Save to search" on document-overview.html. Also
      // schedules a floating-position recalc, since the card just grew
      // taller (whether from the form being revealed, or from a reply
      // being added) and may now overlap the one below it.
      function wireCommentReply (card) {
        var showReplyLink = card.querySelector('[data-show-reply-form]')
        var replyForm = card.querySelector('[data-reply-form]')
        var addReplyButton = card.querySelector('[data-add-reply]')
        var replyInput = card.querySelector('[data-reply-input]')
        var repliesList = card.querySelector('[data-replies-list]')

        if (showReplyLink && replyForm) {
          showReplyLink.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
            event.stopPropagation()
            replyForm.hidden = false
            showReplyLink.hidden = true
            if (replyInput) replyInput.focus()
            scheduleFloatingRecalc()
          })
        }

        if (!addReplyButton || !replyInput || !repliesList) return

        addReplyButton.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
          event.stopPropagation()
          var text = replyInput.value.trim()
          if (!text) return

          var item = document.createElement('li')
          item.className = 'app-experiment-comment__reply'
          item.innerHTML =
            '<p class="govuk-body-s app-experiment-comment__meta">You &middot; Just now</p>' +
            '<p class="govuk-body app-experiment-comment__text">' + escapeHtml(text) + '</p>'
          repliesList.appendChild(item)

          replyInput.value = ''
          scheduleFloatingRecalc()

          var commentId = card.dataset.commentId
          if (commentId && window.fetch) {
            fetch('/playground/editor/reply', {
              method: 'POST',
              headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
              body: JSON.stringify({ commentId: commentId, text: text })
            }).catch(function () {})
          }
        })
      }

      // Removes the whole card (and its full thread — a reply only ever
      // exists nested inside its own parent's data-replies-list, there is
      // nothing else pointing at it to clean up) from the panel instantly,
      // and fires a background POST so it stays gone after a reload — see
      // getEditorExperimentDeletedCommentIds/buildEditorExperimentComments
      // and POST /playground/editor/delete in app/routes.js.
      // event.stopPropagation() keeps this click from also reaching the
      // header's own highlight listener (Delete sits inside that same
      // header, on the meta row) — deleting a comment should not also
      // scroll the editor away first. Also schedules a floating-position
      // recalc, since removing a card frees up space the ones below it
      // (if any) can now move back up into.
      function wireCommentDelete (card) {
        var deleteButton = card.querySelector('[data-delete-comment]')
        if (!deleteButton) return

        deleteButton.addEventListener('click', function (event) {
          event.stopPropagation()
          var commentId = card.dataset.commentId
          card.remove()

          // Drop this card from floatingCards too, if it was in there —
          // otherwise computeFloatingPositions() (see scheduleFloatingRecalc
          // below) would keep reserving space for a card that no longer
          // exists, pushing the ones after it down further than they need
          // to be.
          var floatingIndex = floatingCards.findIndex(function (entry) { return entry.card === card })
          if (floatingIndex !== -1) floatingCards.splice(floatingIndex, 1)

          scheduleFloatingRecalc()
          updateTabCounts()

          if (commentId && window.fetch) {
            fetch('/playground/editor/delete', {
              method: 'POST',
              headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
              body: JSON.stringify({ commentId: commentId })
            }).catch(function () {})
          }
        })
      }

      document.querySelectorAll('.app-experiment-comment').forEach(wireCommentCard)

      // Word-style floating comments, in normal document flow — not
      // position: absolute (an earlier version was; a wrong/stale
      // computed `top` for one card could then render its entire
      // contents, Add button included, on top of a different card's
      // text instead of below it, which is the bug this rebuild removes
      // the *possibility* of, not just the specific instance of).
      //
      // floatingCards pairs each app-experiment-comment--floating card
      // with the app-editor-anchor span sharing its data-anchor-id
      // (comment.anchorId, app/routes.js, for the three seeded comments
      // and any restored via restoreAddedCommentAnchors above) — built
      // once at load. computeFloatingPositions() below walks this array
      // in order, computing each card's margin-top relative to the one
      // before it, which only gives correct results if the array is
      // already sorted top-to-bottom by anchor position — true for the
      // three seeded cards (anchor-1/2/3 sit in section-1/2/3 in that
      // order already), so no sorting is needed for those, unlike the
      // absolute-position version this replaced. A comment created live
      // afterwards is NOT simply appended to the end: insertFloatingCard
      // (further down, used by the selection-trigger click handler)
      // finds where its anchor actually falls relative to the existing
      // ones and splices it in at the matching index instead — appending
      // one whose anchor is earlier in the document than an existing
      // entry would otherwise feed computeFloatingPositions a negative
      // gap, which Math.max(0, ...) would just floor to "sits right
      // below the previous card", not anywhere near its own anchor.
      //
      // computeFloatingPositions() sets each card's own margin-top —
      // nothing else — to roughly line it up with its anchor:
      //   - the FIRST card gets a margin-top equal to its anchor's
      //     position relative to the *first* card's own natural position
      //     (editorRect/headerOffset corrects for the Comments column's
      //     header not necessarily being the same height as the editor's
      //     toolbar, the same correction the old version used, just
      //     applied once here instead of to every card).
      //   - every card after that gets a margin-top equal to
      //     (gap between this anchor and the previous one) minus (the
      //     previous card's own rendered height) — i.e. just enough extra
      //     space, on top of the margin-bottom every card already has, to
      //     roughly close the same gap the anchors themselves have.
      // Math.max(0, ...) on every one of these is what actually prevents
      // overlap: a margin can't go negative here, so a card can never be
      // pulled up into the one before it — this is normal CSS block flow
      // guaranteeing no overlap between siblings, not the algorithm
      // "happening" to avoid it. When anchors sit closer together than
      // the cards referring to them are tall, the margin simply floors at
      // 0 and the cards just stack with their ordinary spacing, no longer
      // aligned to their anchors but never overlapping each other either.
      //
      // Both the anchor and the card move together by the same amount as
      // the page scrolls (neither the editor pane nor the Comments panel
      // has its own independent scroll — see .app-experiment-box in
      // _app-editor-experiment.scss), so none of this needs to run again
      // on scroll for correctness; the listeners below exist for when a
      // card's own height or an anchor's position on the page can
      // actually change instead (window resize; adding/deleting a
      // comment, via the explicit scheduleFloatingRecalc() calls in
      // wireCommentReply/wireCommentDelete above; and once on load).
      function findAnchorSpan (anchorId) {
        return document.querySelector('.app-editor-anchor[data-anchor-id="' + anchorId + '"]')
      }

      var floatingCards = []
      document.querySelectorAll('.app-experiment-comment--floating').forEach(function (card) {
        var anchor = findAnchorSpan(card.dataset.anchorId)
        if (anchor) floatingCards.push({ card: card, anchor: anchor })
      })

      function computeFloatingPositions () {
        // Comments now sit in the right-hand panel, which scrolls separately
        // from the editor, so cards stack rather than lining up with anchors.
        return
        if (!floatingCards.length || !editorContent) return

        var editorRect = editorContent.getBoundingClientRect()
        var listRect = floatingCards[0].card.parentElement.getBoundingClientRect()
        var headerOffset = listRect.top - editorRect.top

        var firstAnchorRect = floatingCards[0].anchor.getBoundingClientRect()
        var firstRawTop = (firstAnchorRect.top - editorRect.top) - headerOffset
        floatingCards[0].card.style.marginTop = Math.max(0, firstRawTop) + 'px'

        var previousAnchorTop = firstAnchorRect.top
        var previousCard = floatingCards[0].card

        for (var i = 1; i < floatingCards.length; i++) {
          var entry = floatingCards[i]
          var anchorRect = entry.anchor.getBoundingClientRect()
          var anchorGap = anchorRect.top - previousAnchorTop
          var extra = anchorGap - previousCard.offsetHeight
          entry.card.style.marginTop = Math.max(0, extra) + 'px'

          previousAnchorTop = anchorRect.top
          previousCard = entry.card
        }
      }

      // Inserts a newly created {card, anchor} pair into floatingCards
      // (and card itself into the DOM) at the position matching where
      // anchor's current viewport position actually falls relative to
      // the anchors already in the array — not at the end — which is
      // what keeps floatingCards sorted top-to-bottom the way
      // computeFloatingPositions above requires. Used by the
      // selection-trigger click handler further up (the only place a new
      // floating card is ever created after load).
      function insertFloatingCard (card, anchor) {
        var newTop = anchor.getBoundingClientRect().top
        var insertIndex = floatingCards.length

        for (var i = 0; i < floatingCards.length; i++) {
          if (floatingCards[i].anchor.getBoundingClientRect().top > newTop) {
            insertIndex = i
            break
          }
        }

        var floatingList = document.querySelector('[data-comments-list]')
        if (floatingList) {
          var referenceCard = insertIndex < floatingCards.length ? floatingCards[insertIndex].card : null
          if (referenceCard) {
            floatingList.insertBefore(card, referenceCard)
          } else {
            floatingList.appendChild(card)
          }
        }

        floatingCards.splice(insertIndex, 0, { card: card, anchor: anchor })
      }

      var floatingRecalcScheduled = false
      function scheduleFloatingRecalc () {
        if (floatingRecalcScheduled || !floatingCards.length) return
        floatingRecalcScheduled = true
        window.requestAnimationFrame(function () {
          floatingRecalcScheduled = false
          computeFloatingPositions()
        })
      }

      if (floatingCards.length) {
        window.addEventListener('resize', scheduleFloatingRecalc)
        scheduleFloatingRecalc()
      }

      // Clicking an anchor's icon scrolls the editor pane so that exact
      // text is back on screen — which, via the scroll listener just
      // above, naturally brings its floating comment card back into view
      // too, smoothly, with no separate "flag this card" step needed (the
      // previous IntersectionObserver-driven highlight/flagging this
      // replaced worked independently of position; position now carries
      // that meaning by itself). It also flashes the anchor span itself
      // (the specific sentence the comment refers to — more precise than
      // wireHighlightTrigger's whole-section flash above, matching how
      // this already scrolls to the anchor itself, not its section) and,
      // if it can find one, that anchor's own comment card, using
      // flashHighlight above — same as clicking the card itself would.
      document.querySelectorAll('[data-anchor-icon]').forEach(function (icon) {
        icon.addEventListener('click', function () {
          var anchor = findAnchorSpan(icon.dataset.anchorIcon)
          var card = document.querySelector('.app-experiment-comment[data-anchor-id="' + icon.dataset.anchorIcon + '"]')

          flashHighlight(anchor, card)
          if (anchor) anchor.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'center' })
        })
      })

      // "Add new content": reveals the input+Add row below "Add new
      // content" itself (which hides, same reveal-and-stay pattern as
      // above) — submitting appends a new item to the Content nav list
      // and a matching new, empty heading (a boundary marker included, so
      // it looks exactly like every other section) to the very end of the
      // editor content. Both the nav link and the new heading use the
      // entered text verbatim; ids are generated locally
      // ('section-new-N') and never collide with the fixed sample's own
      // section-1..6 or a real document's editorSections ids, which are
      // never named that way (see buildEditorSections, app/routes.js).
      var addContentLink = document.querySelector('[data-show-add-content]')
      var addContentForm = document.querySelector('[data-add-content-form]')
      var addContentInput = document.querySelector('[data-add-content-input]')
      var addContentButton = document.querySelector('[data-add-content-button]')
      var contentNavList = document.querySelector('[data-content-nav-list]')
      var localSectionCounter = 0

      if (addContentLink && addContentForm) {
        addContentLink.addEventListener('click', function () {
          addContentForm.hidden = false
          addContentLink.hidden = true
          if (addContentInput) addContentInput.focus()
        })
      }

      if (addContentButton && addContentInput && contentNavList && editorContent) {
        addContentButton.addEventListener('click', function () {
          var label = addContentInput.value.trim()
          if (!label) return

          var id = 'section-new-' + (localSectionCounter += 1)

          var li = document.createElement('li')
          li.className = 'app-experiment-nav__item'
          li.innerHTML =
            '<button type="button" class="app-experiment-nav__handle" draggable="true" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true">' +
              '{{ ttIcon("drag") }}' +
            '</button>' +
            '<a class="govuk-link app-experiment-nav__link" href="#' + id + '" data-highlight-trigger data-target-section="' + id + '">' + escapeHtml(label) + '</a>'
          contentNavList.appendChild(li)
          wireHighlightTrigger(li.querySelector('[data-highlight-trigger]'))

          var boundary = document.createElement('div')
          boundary.className = 'app-experiment-boundary'
          boundary.setAttribute('data-boundary', '')
          boundary.innerHTML =
            '<span class="app-experiment-boundary__label">Start of ' + escapeHtml(label) + '</span>' +
            '<div class="app-experiment-boundary__line"></div>' +
            '<button type="button" class="app-experiment-boundary__handle" data-boundary-handle aria-label="Drag, or use the up and down arrow keys, to move paragraphs between this section and the one before it" tabindex="0"></button>'
          editorContent.appendChild(boundary)
          wireBoundaryHandle(boundary.querySelector('[data-boundary-handle]'))

          var section = document.createElement('div')
          section.id = id
          section.innerHTML = '<h2 class="govuk-heading-m">' + escapeHtml(label) + '</h2>'
          editorContent.appendChild(section)

          addContentInput.value = ''
        })
      }

      // Content nav drag-to-reorder — visual/list-order only, same scope
      // limit as the boundary markers above: dragging a card here moves
      // its <li> within this <ul> and nothing else. It never touches
      // editorContent, so the actual section-N divs (and the order
      // they're written in) are completely unaffected — reordering the
      // list does NOT reorder the document. This is deliberately decoupled
      // for this iteration, not a step toward a full implementation.
      //
      // Native HTML5 drag-and-drop (draggable="true" + dragstart/
      // dragover/dragend), not a hand-rolled mouse-tracking version like
      // wireBoundaryHandle below — reordering a list of siblings is
      // exactly what that API is for, and it needs no custom collision
      // math (browsers fire dragover on whichever element the pointer is
      // currently over). draggable="true" sits on each item's own
      // app-experiment-nav__handle (the grip icon), not the <li> itself,
      // so a drag can only start from the handle — clicking the link
      // text elsewhere on the card still just navigates, never drags.
      //
      // Wired once via event delegation on the list itself (dragstart/
      // dragover/dragend all bubble), so a card added later through "Add
      // new content" above — built with the same handle markup — is
      // already draggable with no extra wiring call needed, the same way
      // wireHighlightTrigger has to be called explicitly for each new
      // element but this doesn't.
      //
      // GDS deviation to flag: this has no keyboard equivalent (the
      // handle is tabindex="-1"/aria-hidden, deliberately not reachable
      // by keyboard) — drag-and-drop reordering with no keyboard path is
      // a known accessibility gap. A real implementation would need an
      // alternative (e.g. "Move up"/"Move down" buttons, or arrow-key
      // reordering once a handle has focus) before this could ship;
      // building that wasn't asked for here.
      function wireNavDragAndDrop () {
        var navList = document.querySelector('[data-content-nav-list]')
        if (!navList) return

        var draggedItem = null

        navList.addEventListener('dragstart', function (event) {
          var item = event.target.closest('.app-experiment-nav__item')
          if (!item) return
          draggedItem = item
          item.classList.add('app-experiment-nav__item--dragging')
          if (event.dataTransfer) {
            event.dataTransfer.effectAllowed = 'move'
            // Firefox requires data to actually be set for the drag to proceed.
            event.dataTransfer.setData('text/plain', '')
          }
        })

        navList.addEventListener('dragover', function (event) {
          if (!draggedItem) return
          event.preventDefault() // required to allow a drop at all

          var target = event.target.closest('.app-experiment-nav__item')
          if (!target || target === draggedItem) return

          var targetRect = target.getBoundingClientRect()
          var insertBefore = (event.clientY - targetRect.top) < (targetRect.height / 2)
          navList.insertBefore(draggedItem, insertBefore ? target : target.nextSibling)
        })

        navList.addEventListener('dragend', function () {
          if (draggedItem) draggedItem.classList.remove('app-experiment-nav__item--dragging')
          draggedItem = null
        })
      }

      wireNavDragAndDrop()

      // A section-N div's own heading is always its first child (every
      // render path — the fixed sample, the editorSections loop,
      // restoredEditorHtml and "Add new content" above — writes it that
      // way) so "everything after the heading" is exactly that section's
      // body content: shared by the boundary-drag reassignment below and
      // buildCustomStepsFromEditor's own body-extraction, so both agree on
      // what counts as one movable "paragraph" without maintaining two
      // separate definitions of it.
      function getSectionBodyNodes (sectionEl) {
        return Array.prototype.slice.call(sectionEl.children, 1)
      }

      // A single shared, screen-reader-only status region (created once,
      // lazily) announcing a successful drag/keyboard reassignment below —
      // there is no visible toast for this: the moved paragraph physically
      // relocating between sections on screen already is the visible
      // confirmation, this just gives an equivalent confirmation to anyone
      // not watching the screen, the same role="status" pattern
      // saved-document-view.html's own "Your place has been saved" uses.
      var boundaryStatus = null

      function announceBoundaryMove (movedCount, fromHeading, toHeading) {
        if (!boundaryStatus) {
          boundaryStatus = document.createElement('div')
          boundaryStatus.className = 'govuk-visually-hidden'
          boundaryStatus.setAttribute('role', 'status')
          editorContent.parentNode.insertBefore(boundaryStatus, editorContent)
        }
        boundaryStatus.textContent =
          'Moved ' + movedCount + (movedCount === 1 ? ' paragraph' : ' paragraphs') +
          ' from ' + fromHeading + ' to ' + toHeading + '.'
      }

      // Functional section boundaries — GOV.UK Design System has no
      // equivalent component for this (a divider you drag to reassign
      // document content between two sections); flagged here the same way
      // the Tiptap-styled toolbar's own deviations were, and still true now
      // this is no longer just a visual demo of the interaction.
      //
      // A boundary only ever exchanges paragraphs between the TWO sections
      // it sits directly between (previousElementSibling/nextElementSibling
      // — boundary markers and section-N divs strictly alternate in the
      // DOM) — headings never move, only body nodes (getSectionBodyNodes
      // above) do, and only ever across this one boundary, never further.
      // There is no separate JS model of "which section a paragraph
      // belongs to" anywhere on this page: it is simply which section-N
      // div a paragraph is a DOM child of, the same way Content-nav
      // drag-and-drop and "Add new content" already treat the DOM itself
      // as the source of truth — so reassignment here IS just moving the
      // real nodes (appendChild), not a visual-only stand-in for it, and
      // buildCustomStepsFromEditor (reading the DOM fresh on every Preview
      // click) automatically reflects the result with no extra syncing.
      //
      // Valid drop points ("gaps") are computed fresh on every mousedown/
      // keydown — not cached — since an earlier drag on this or another
      // boundary may already have changed either section's paragraph
      // count. A gap can only sit strictly between two paragraphs (drawn
      // from the combined, in-order list of the section above's body nodes
      // followed by the section below's), one boundary position per pair
      // of adjacent paragraphs and never mid-paragraph — the two outermost
      // possible positions (before the very first combined paragraph, or
      // after the very last) are deliberately never included, since either
      // would leave one of the two sections with zero paragraphs; if that
      // leaves no candidate gaps at all (each side already down to exactly
      // one paragraph — or the very first boundary in the document, which
      // has no section above it to exchange with at all) the drag/keypress
      // is simply blocked, same as any other out-of-range attempt below.
      function wireBoundaryHandle (handle) {
        if (!handle) return
        var boundary = handle.closest('[data-boundary]')
        if (!boundary) return

        var sectionAbove = boundary.previousElementSibling
        var sectionBelow = boundary.nextElementSibling
        var dragging = false
        var gaps = []
        var combinedNodes = []
        var originalKeepAboveCount = 0
        var activeGap = null

        function computeGaps () {
          gaps = []
          combinedNodes = []
          if (!sectionAbove || !sectionBelow) return

          var aboveNodes = getSectionBodyNodes(sectionAbove)
          var belowNodes = getSectionBodyNodes(sectionBelow)
          combinedNodes = aboveNodes.concat(belowNodes)
          originalKeepAboveCount = aboveNodes.length

          for (var keepAboveCount = 1; keepAboveCount < combinedNodes.length; keepAboveCount++) {
            var beforeRect = combinedNodes[keepAboveCount - 1].getBoundingClientRect()
            var afterRect = combinedNodes[keepAboveCount].getBoundingClientRect()
            gaps.push({ keepAboveCount: keepAboveCount, y: (beforeRect.bottom + afterRect.top) / 2 })
          }
        }

        function nearestGap (y) {
          var nearest = null
          var nearestDistance = Infinity
          gaps.forEach(function (gap) {
            var distance = Math.abs(gap.y - y)
            if (distance < nearestDistance) {
              nearestDistance = distance
              nearest = gap
            }
          })
          return nearest
        }

        function previewGap (gap) {
          if (!gap) {
            boundary.style.transform = ''
            return
          }
          var boundaryRect = boundary.getBoundingClientRect()
          var boundaryMid = (boundaryRect.top + boundaryRect.bottom) / 2
          boundary.style.transform = 'translateY(' + (gap.y - boundaryMid) + 'px)'
        }

        // The only step that actually rewrites section membership — every
        // other function here (computeGaps/nearestGap/previewGap) just
        // works out where a drop WOULD land and previews it visually.
        function commitGap (gap) {
          boundary.style.transform = ''
          if (!gap || !combinedNodes.length || gap.keepAboveCount === originalKeepAboveCount) return

          var movedCount = Math.abs(gap.keepAboveCount - originalKeepAboveCount)
          var direction = gap.keepAboveCount > originalKeepAboveCount ? 'down-to-up' : 'up-to-down'

          combinedNodes.forEach(function (node) { node.parentNode.removeChild(node) })
          combinedNodes.slice(0, gap.keepAboveCount).forEach(function (node) { sectionAbove.appendChild(node) })
          combinedNodes.slice(gap.keepAboveCount).forEach(function (node) { sectionBelow.appendChild(node) })

          var aboveHeading = sectionAbove.children[0] ? sectionAbove.children[0].textContent.trim() : ''
          var belowHeading = sectionBelow.children[0] ? sectionBelow.children[0].textContent.trim() : ''
          announceBoundaryMove(
            movedCount,
            direction === 'up-to-down' ? aboveHeading : belowHeading,
            direction === 'up-to-down' ? belowHeading : aboveHeading
          )

          scheduleFloatingRecalc()
        }

        function onMouseMove (event) {
          if (!dragging) return
          activeGap = nearestGap(event.clientY)
          previewGap(activeGap)
        }

        function onMouseUp () {
          dragging = false
          document.removeEventListener('mousemove', onMouseMove)
          document.removeEventListener('mouseup', onMouseUp)
          commitGap(activeGap)
        }

        handle.addEventListener('mousedown', function (event) {
          if (!sectionAbove || !sectionBelow) return
          computeGaps()
          if (!gaps.length) return
          event.preventDefault()
          dragging = true
          activeGap = null
          document.addEventListener('mousemove', onMouseMove)
          document.addEventListener('mouseup', onMouseUp)
        })

        handle.addEventListener('keydown', function (event) {
          if (event.key !== 'ArrowUp' && event.key !== 'ArrowDown') return
          if (!sectionAbove || !sectionBelow) return
          event.preventDefault()

          computeGaps()
          if (!gaps.length) return

          var targetKeepAboveCount = originalKeepAboveCount + (event.key === 'ArrowUp' ? -1 : 1)
          var gap = gaps.filter(function (candidate) { return candidate.keepAboveCount === targetKeepAboveCount })[0]
          if (!gap) return

          commitGap(gap)
        })
      }

      document.querySelectorAll('[data-boundary-handle]').forEach(wireBoundaryHandle)

      // "Preview" — posts the editor's current live content (including any
      // unsaved edits and reordering, since nothing here is persisted until
      // "Save") into req.session.data.editorExperimentPreview, then
      // navigates to /playground/editor/preview, which renders the REAL
      // saved-document-view.html customSteps stepper from that session data
      // (see routes.js) — the same template/branch a document like
      // cs-ma-land-user-or-land-cover-not-compatible-signoff-2026 already
      // uses, not a simplified alternate layout. Two things are sent: "html"
      // is an exact, untouched copy of [data-editor-content], used to put
      // the editor back exactly as it was when "Back to editor" is used;
      // "steps" is the same content split into one customSteps-shaped entry
      // per Content-nav section (see buildCustomStepsFromEditor below), so
      // it renders as "Part 1 of N: <section>", "Part 2 of N: ...", etc.
      // instead of one long scrolling page.
      var previewButton = document.querySelector('[data-preview-button]')

      // One customSteps entry per <li> in the Content nav, in ITS current
      // DOM order — the same order wireNavDragAndDrop leaves it in after any
      // drag-to-reorder, and including anything appended by "Add new
      // content" — rather than iterating editorContent's own section-N divs
      // directly, since the nav list (not document order) is this page's
      // one definition of "the current section order". sectionName comes
      // from the nav link's own (static) label; heading comes from the
      // section's own <h2> as it stands right now, since that IS
      // live-editable text and can have been changed since the nav link was
      // first rendered — matching how a real document's own flat steps
      // (app/data/guidance-documents.js) can have a sectionName that
      // differs from its numbered heading.
      //
      // body is built generically from the section's own child elements
      // (skipping its <h2>): a <ul>/<ol> becomes a bullet-list entry (one
      // string per <li>), anything else (a <p>, or an <h3> sub-heading such
      // as the fixed sample's "Land eligibility"/"Business eligibility")
      // becomes a plain-paragraph string. This intentionally uses
      // .textContent, not .innerHTML — the customSteps body shape used by
      // real documents is plain text (each entry piped through
      // renderLinkMarkers, which HTML-escapes everything except its own
      // LINK-marker markup — see app/filters.js), so this reuses that
      // exact rendering path rather than special-casing preview-sourced
      // steps to accept raw HTML. The one consequence: inline formatting
      // applied via the toolbar (bold/italic) and an <h3> sub-heading's
      // extra emphasis do not carry into the preview, only their text and
      // paragraph/bullet-list structure do. .textContent also naturally
      // drops the comment-anchor icon buttons (SVG-only, no text) and
      // resolves an app-editor-anchor span to just its wrapped text, with
      // no separate stripping step needed.
      function buildCustomStepsFromEditor () {
        var navItems = document.querySelectorAll('[data-content-nav-list] > li')
        var steps = []

        navItems.forEach(function (item) {
          var link = item.querySelector('.app-experiment-nav__link')
          if (!link) return

          var sectionId = link.getAttribute('href').slice(1)
          var sectionEl = document.getElementById(sectionId)
          if (!sectionEl) return

          var headingEl = sectionEl.children[0]
          var heading = headingEl ? headingEl.textContent.trim() : link.textContent.trim()
          var body = []

          getSectionBodyNodes(sectionEl).forEach(function (child) {
            if (child.tagName === 'UL' || child.tagName === 'OL') {
              var items = Array.prototype.map.call(child.querySelectorAll('li'), function (li) {
                return li.textContent.trim()
              }).filter(function (text) { return text })
              if (items.length) body.push(items)
              return
            }

            var text = child.textContent.trim()
            if (text) body.push(text)
          })

          steps.push({
            sectionName: link.textContent.trim(),
            heading: heading,
            body: body
          })
        })

        return steps
      }

      if (previewButton) {
        previewButton.addEventListener('click', function () {
          var params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search)
          var heading = document.querySelector('h1.govuk-heading-l')

          fetch('/playground/editor/preview', {
            method: 'POST',
            headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
            body: JSON.stringify({
              id: params.get('id') || '',
              title: heading ? heading.textContent.trim() : '',
              html: editorContent.innerHTML,
              steps: buildCustomStepsFromEditor()
            })
          }).then(function () {
            window.location.href = '/playground/editor/preview'
          })
        })
      }

      // "Send for approval" — captures the same draft as Preview (same
      // endpoint, same payload shape), then navigates to the review step
      // (send-for-approval.njk) rather than changing anything immediately;
      // that page's own "Send for approval" button is what makes the real
      // state change. See the template comment above this button.
      var sendForApprovalButton = document.querySelector('[data-send-for-approval-button]')

      if (sendForApprovalButton) {
        sendForApprovalButton.addEventListener('click', function () {
          var params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search)
          var heading = document.querySelector('h1.govuk-heading-l')

          fetch('/playground/editor/preview', {
            method: 'POST',
            headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
            body: JSON.stringify({
              id: params.get('id') || '',
              title: heading ? heading.textContent.trim() : '',
              html: editorContent.innerHTML,
              steps: buildCustomStepsFromEditor()
            })
          }).then(function () {
            window.location.href = '/playground/editor/send-for-approval'
          })
        })
      }
    })()
  </script>
{% endblock %}
